================================================================================
CORE MODULE RESPONSIBILITIES
================================================================================

The core/ folder contains code that belongs to no single OEM. Any function that
two or more OEM pipelines would otherwise duplicate belongs here.

------------------------------------------------------------
core/helpers/header_helpers.py
------------------------------------------------------------
  promote_header_row(df) -> df
    Drops row 0, resets index, promotes new row 0 as column headers.
    Returns a copy — never mutates the input df.

  lowercase_columns(col_list: list) -> list
    Lowercases all strings in col_list, preserving order.

------------------------------------------------------------
core/helpers/column_mapper.py
------------------------------------------------------------
  column_type_finder(col_name, column_definition) -> dict
    Matches a single column name against the column_definition config.
    Returns {"original": "mapped_name"} or {"original": None}.
    Rules applied in sequence: not_have -> must_have -> must_have_one_of.
    All matching is case-insensitive.

  map_all_columns(df, column_definition) -> dict
    Calls column_type_finder for every column in df.
    Returns the full mapping dict for the sheet.

  assert_required_columns(col_mapping, required_cols) -> None or raises FATAL
    Verifies that every entry in required_cols has a non-None match in
    col_mapping. Raises FATAL listing any missing columns.

------------------------------------------------------------
core/helpers/trim_helpers.py
------------------------------------------------------------
  identify_trim_candidates(df, trim_bounds_config) -> list
    Locates left and right boundary columns using the bounds config.
    Returns the list of column names strictly between the two bounds.
    Raises FATAL if either boundary cannot be found.

  validate_trim_by_datatype(df, candidates, trim_validation_config) -> (list, dict)
    Validates each candidate column against the trim_validation_config.
    Resolves "__na__" sentinel to pd.isna() checks.
    All string comparisons are case-insensitive.
    Returns (passed_cols_list, results_dict).

------------------------------------------------------------
core/helpers/dq_logger.py
------------------------------------------------------------
  Audience: business stakeholders / data stewards.
  Purpose:  tracks issues found WITHIN the data (DQ_WARNINGs only).
            Produces the DQ report that goes to output/dq_reports/.

  DQLogger class
    Instantiated once per pipeline run with a run_id and source_file.
    Accumulates DQ_WARNING records during the run.

    Methods:
      log_warning(sheet_name, model_name, record_index, record_snapshot,
                  rule_violated, issue_description)
        Appends a DQ_WARNING record to the internal log.

      write_dq_report(output_path)
        Writes the full record-level DQ log and a per-sheet summary
        (total records, records excluded, warning counts by rule) to
        output_path.

------------------------------------------------------------
core/helpers/pipeline_logger.py
------------------------------------------------------------
  Audience: developers / ops.
  Purpose:  tracks what the pipeline DID during a run — FATAL errors,
            sheet skips, files processed, record counts, run timing.
            Built on Python's standard logging module.
            Writes to output/pipeline_logs/<oem>/<run_id>.log

  PipelineLogger class
    Wraps Python's logging.Logger. Instantiated once per run.

    Methods:
      log_run_start(oem, file_path)
        Records run start time and source file.

      log_sheet_start(sheet_name)
        Records that processing has begun for a sheet.

      log_fatal(sheet_name, step, reason)
        Records a FATAL event and the reason the sheet was skipped.
        Does not stop execution — the orchestrator handles the skip
        after calling this.

      log_sheet_complete(sheet_name, records_in, records_out)
        Records record counts when a sheet finishes successfully.

      log_run_complete()
        Records run end time and total sheets processed / skipped.

------------------------------------------------------------
core/base_pipeline.py
------------------------------------------------------------
  BasePipeline (abstract class)
    Defines the contract that every OEM orchestrator must follow.
    Each step is a method with a defined signature. OEM pipelines inherit
    from this class and implement or override each step.

    Abstract methods (must be implemented per OEM):
      run_step1_validation(df, config, meta_data, dq_logger, pipeline_logger) -> working_df
      run_step2_header_normalization(working_df, config, dq_logger, pipeline_logger) -> step2_result
      run_step3_standardization(working_df, step2_result, config, pipeline_logger) -> standardized_df
      run_step4_transformation(standardized_df, step2_result, dq_logger, pipeline_logger) -> transformed_df
      run_step5_output(transformed_df, meta_data, config, pipeline_logger) -> None

    Concrete method (shared logic, not overridden):
      run(file_path, config)
        The main entry point. Instantiates both loggers, loads the Excel
        file, iterates over sheets, calls each step in sequence, handles
        FATAL errors (skip sheet), and writes both logs at the end.

------------------------------------------------------------
core/config_loader.py
------------------------------------------------------------
  load_config(config_path) -> dict
    Loads and parses the OEM's JSON config file.
    Validates that all required top-level keys are present.
    Raises an error on load if the config is structurally invalid —
    this fails fast before any data is touched.

  Required top-level keys every config must have:
    column_definition, required_columns, non_null_columns,
    col_data_type_dict, trim_bounds_config, trim_validation_config,
    non_acceptable_sheet_names, output


================================================================================
OEM-SPECIFIC MODULE RESPONSIBILITIES
================================================================================

Each OEM lives under oems/<oem_name>/. An OEM folder contains:

  config/<oem>_config.json
    The single config file for this OEM. Follows the standard config schema
    (see below). All OEM-specific business rules live here, not in code.

  pipeline/orchestrator.py
    Entry point for this OEM. Inherits from BasePipeline and implements
    each step. If an OEM's step logic is identical to another OEM's, both
    can call the same shared helper — the difference lives in the config.

  pipeline/step1_validation.py  (and steps 2–5)
    Individual step implementations. For simple OEMs these may just call
    core helpers directly. For OEMs with unusual file structures, this is
    where custom logic lives.

  tests/
    Unit tests for this OEM's pipeline steps, using sample input files.

Key principle: if a step implementation is the same across two OEMs, do NOT
copy the code. Instead, move that logic into core/helpers/ and have both
OEMs call it. The only thing that should differ per OEM is config values and
any genuinely unique file-structure handling.


================================================================================
STANDARD CONFIG SCHEMA
================================================================================

Every OEM config file must follow this schema. Optional sections are noted.

{
  "column_definition": {
    "<standard_col_name>": {
      "key_words": {
        "must_have":        [...],
        "must_have_one_of": [...],
        "not_have":         [...]
      }
    }
  },

  "required_columns":           [...list of standard_col_names that must be found],
  "non_null_columns":           [...list of standard_col_names that must not be null],

  "col_data_type_dict": {
    "to_string":  [...col names],
    "to_float":   [...col names]
  },

  "trim_bounds_config": {
    "left_bound":  { "must_contain": [...], "must_contain_one_of": [...] },
    "right_bound": { "must_contain": [...], "must_contain_one_of": [...] }
  },

  "trim_validation_config": {
    "expected_data":  { "data": "X", "perc_thresh": 60 },
    "must_have_only": { "expected_value_types": ["X", "", "__na__"] }
  },

  "non_acceptable_sheet_names": [...],

  "output": {
    "ready_to_upload_path": "output/ready_to_upload/<oem>/",
    "dq_report_path":       "output/dq_reports/<oem>/",
    "pipeline_log_path":    "output/pipeline_logs/<oem>/"
  }
}

Notes:
  - All string comparisons in the pipeline are case-insensitive by default.
  - "__na__" in expected_value_types resolves to pd.isna() at runtime.
  - If an OEM does not use trim levels, trim_bounds_config and
    trim_validation_config can be set to null and step 4 is skipped.


================================================================================
HOW TO ONBOARD A NEW OEM
================================================================================

To add a new OEM to the pipeline:

  1. Create the folder:
       oems/<new_oem>/config/
       oems/<new_oem>/pipeline/
       oems/<new_oem>/tests/

  2. Create oems/<new_oem>/config/<new_oem>_config.json
     Fill in the standard config schema for this OEM's column names,
     required fields, trim boundaries, and output paths.

  3. Create oems/<new_oem>/pipeline/orchestrator.py
     Inherit from BasePipeline. Implement each step method.
     For steps with no OEM-specific logic, call the core helpers directly.
     Override only the steps that need custom handling.

  4. Add a data/landing_zone/<new_oem>/ folder for incoming files.

  5. Run tests against a sample file before connecting to production data.

No changes to core/ are needed unless this OEM requires a capability that
doesn't exist yet (e.g. a new file format). In that case, add the new
capability to core/helpers/ so all OEMs can use it.


================================================================================
PIPELINE EXECUTION FLOW (ALL OEMs)
================================================================================

The run() method in BasePipeline follows this flow for every OEM:

  Logger key:
    [PL] -> pipeline_logger  (execution events — developer-facing)
    [DQ] -> dq_logger        (data quality issues — stakeholder-facing)

  config          = load_config("<oem>_config.json")
  dq_logger       = DQLogger(run_id, source_file)
  pipeline_logger = PipelineLogger(run_id, config["output"]["pipeline_log_path"])

  [PL] log_run_start(oem, file_path)

  For each Excel file in data/landing_zone/<oem>/:
    For each worksheet in the file:

      [PL] log_sheet_start(sheet_name)

      [Step 1]  Validation
                  Sheet name check          [PL] FATAL -> skip sheet
                  Structural validation     [PL] FATAL -> skip sheet
                  Data completeness         [PL] FATAL -> skip sheet
                  Profitability check       [DQ] DQ_WARNING -> log, continue

      [Step 2]  Header normalization
                  Column mapping            [PL] FATAL -> skip sheet
                  Required columns check    [PL] FATAL -> skip sheet
                  Trim candidate detection  [PL] FATAL -> skip sheet
                  Trim validation           [DQ] DQ_WARNING for failed candidates

      [Step 3]  Standardization
                  Promote header            (shared helper)
                  Apply column mapping
                  Enforce data types        [PL] FATAL -> skip sheet
                  Standardize trim values

      [Step 4]  Transformation
                  Applicability validation  [DQ] DQ_WARNING -> exclude record
                  Melt trim columns

      [Step 5]  Output
                  Enrich with metadata
                  Write to output path

      [PL] log_sheet_complete(sheet_name, records_in, records_out)

    End for each worksheet.

    [DQ] dq_logger.write_dq_report(config["output"]["dq_report_path"])
    [PL] pipeline_logger.log_run_complete()

  End for each file.


================================================================================
RELATIONSHIP TO EXISTING V1 CODE
================================================================================

v1 (OEM Accessories_v1/) remains unchanged and in production.
v2 is built alongside it.

Migration path per OEM:
  1. Port the OEM's config values into the new standard config schema.
  2. Implement the orchestrator using core helpers.
  3. Validate output matches v1 output on the same input files.
  4. Switch landing zone routing to v2 once validated.

Existing v1 assets that map to v2:
  v1 config/<oem>_config.json    ->  v2 oems/<oem>/config/<oem>_config.json
  v1 scripts/<oem>_processor.*  ->  v2 oems/<oem>/pipeline/
  v1 landing_zone/<oem>/        ->  v2 data/landing_zone/<oem>/
  v1 ready_to_upload/<oem>/     ->  v2 output/ready_to_upload/<oem>/
